x\\le 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/1a/1ad54d12cf04e7142b0bcdd78484d9098fd9ca689f464fc5c03f1dbb0671569f.png)\n\nThen, we solve $8x+7\\le -9:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x+7 & \\le -9 \\\\ 8x & \\le -16 \\\\ x & \\le -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/12/12c34a5af230afb788ddd8c689ef0e9b29c5c5adaf35a1e81099453436cf7f3f.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/bc/bc365364192f3784c565ebcb819a5f37885830f93510029647d39883217987cb.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $x\\le 2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115903 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186114355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115903 #edb/ref 1069 "q-55288" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115903 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115903 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following number lines represents the values of $x$ that satisfy $2x\\le 8$ or $3x+4\\ge 7?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115903 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "59f4234d-7f54-5341-b6b4-e5e2ca156720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115903 #edb/ref 1074 "We start by solving $2x\\le 8:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\le 8 \\\\ x & \\le 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/f3/f3c81896babacce5e34f1cbb9f179f6843ae46fa45b8b4399b8f7f8eb11284c8.png)\n\nThen, we solve $3x+4\\ge 7:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+4 & \\ge 7 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 3 \\\\ x & \\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/c9/c9f85fc4864f321722a7cb0b017eab1979aa6a84cd07d08b150b625a1720f25a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/9c/9c045011d942bd93712aa87307994f00d9be9613c6dd547476f4fe7bd1622e19.png)\n\nTherefore, the final solution is $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116243 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116243 #edb/ref 1061 3201 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116243 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cdc53761-47a4-5dc1-a8a0-b21a01b5b35a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116243 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116244 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116244 #edb/ref 1061 3202 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116244 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eef16282-4281-509e-8f6c-106a707fcb20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116244 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115321 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116245 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116244 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116245 #edb/ref 1061 3205 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116245 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a370cd83-ae02-58f0-b487-2c4e2f90db1d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116245 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116246 #edb/ref 1061 3208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116246 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "634432ad-9cd7-5544-b7bb-1b5664235ef4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116246 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116417 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the values of $x$ that satisfy *either* $4x+2 < 6$ *or* $2x \\geq 8.$\n\nTo do this, we solve each inequality individually and then compute the union of the two solutions.\n\nWe start by solving $4x+2 < 6\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+2 & <6 \\\\ 4x & <4 \\\\ x & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/0d/0d36b8e68d9fd0b33b255502860481eeb525b60382029b17434bfb072cc39da2.png)\n\nThen, we solve $2x \\geq 8\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & \\ge 8 \\\\ x & \\ge 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ed/edf5665b7cab7f51e3f252601a96d43c6f0cbfc64e5c9b8da81f0ef09d7b2f5a.png)\n\nTo graph the values of $x$ that satisfy either inequality, we overlay the two graphs on the same number line:\n\n![](images/5c/5c60839a626f2ba8a0fe4a24f4011af0fa52c00d79fbf80039bcfdbdf0a612c7.png)\n\nThus, the final solution is $x < 1$ or $x \\geq 4.$ We can write this using interval notation as\n\n$$\n(-\\infty, 1) \\cup [4,\\infty).\n$$" #edb/ref 1759218604541